WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.740
The idea of making the station more commercially acceptable or accessible . . . Nobody ever came to us when I was there and said,

2
00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:13.160
“Here’s a computer print out and this is what you’re going to play.”

3
00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:17.520
Or even, “This is going to be an incentive cut for you to play.”

4
00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:22.860
That was maybe off on the margins, but I never actually saw anybody do it.

5
00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:33.140
That said, there were times at which they would try and nudge that toward us.

6
00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:41.040
And sometimes they . . . That was one way that they would hire their staff around . . . to try and . . . People like the station manager,

7
00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:47.560
or, I’m sorry, the faculty advisor or LSM would try and sort of work around.

8
00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:54.580
They would hire students who they felt would be a little bit more amenable to commercializing the station.

9
00:00:54.580 --> 00:01:03.940
The Manships bought into the School of Journalism and there was a push from the ’80s to get the School of Journalism more involved with the station.

10
00:01:03.940 --> 00:01:10.840
So that meant that you would have sort of a more commercialized sound coming from the School of Journalism.

11
00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:16.180
They wanted the journalism students to sort of be front and center in terms of air personalities,

12
00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:23.960
in terms of music direction, entertainment, and also in journalism.

13
00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:27.080
And none of that really worked out for them.

14
00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:30.180
There was a lot of pushback to that from the students.

15
00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:39.360
And I think this is where, again, this is where the idea of a more commercial, Top-40 sound was coming from.
